  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure to the north remains an influence keeping most of\nthe area, especially the western half, dry and cooler. A massive,\n990-MB coastal low remains offshore from New England. Streams of\ncloud cover will move overhead from the northeast today and\ntonight until this low moves farther north off the Coast of\nNewfoundland and Nova Scotia in Canada. Isolated showers and\npockets of drizzle cannot be ruled out in the mountains,\nparticularly the northeastern section, as well as northern West\nVirginia. Due to the aridity of our air mass, QPF will be less \nthan a tenth of an inch. \n\nOutside of these slight chances for precipitation, the weather\nwill be mostly dry and quiet through about Thursday afternoon. A\nfront will move up from the south and park over the area\nThursday night. Chances for showers and maybe isolated \nthunderstorms return with this front as it hangs around through\nthe weekend. Confidence is still lacking with models showing a\nlarge divergence in outcomes Thursday through Sunday, but odds\nare favoring a reemergence of a wet pattern. \n\nTemperatures will remain cooler than normal through Monday with\nupper 60s and lower 70s for highs across the lowlands and 50s\nand 60s in the mountains. Lows tonight and Monday night will\ndrop into the 40s and 50s. This morning will may prove to be \nthe coolest with most locations seeing 40s, and cold spots in \nthe northern mountain valleys may reach the upper 30s. Cloud \ncover would be the sole hindrance for this.",
